Minimal, validated in-memory (flips the repro to grounded; a hallucinated control sentence still flags at 0.0). In src/bettermemory/groundedness.py, widen _content_tokens (lines 97-102) with a camel-boundary expansion applied to the RAW text (it must run before tokenize() lowercases, which destroys case information):

_CAMEL_BOUNDARY_RE = re.compile(r"(?<=[a-z0-9])(?=[A-Z])")

def _content_tokens(text: str) -> set[str]:
    toks = tokenize(text)
    spaced = _CAMEL_BOUNDARY_RE.sub(" ", text)
    if spaced != text:
        toks = toks + tokenize(spaced)
    return set(_strip_stopwords(_expand_kebab(toks)))

This preserves the module's conservative design: like _expand_kebab, it keeps the whole token (via the unmodified tokenize(text) pass) and appends the split parts, and since expansion only ADDS tokens to both sides it can only raise overlap ratios — it can never create a new false 'ungrounded' flag. The lookbehind-only-on-[a-z0-9] pattern deliberately under-splits ALLCAPS acronyms (HTMLParser stays whole), matching the module's stated heuristic-not-proof stance. Add two tests to tests/test_groundedness.py: camelCase body vs prose transcript is grounded (the repro), and the hallucinated-sentence control still flags. Optionally apply the same expansion to the index side of search/dedup later for consistency, but that is a separate, larger change; the groundedness fix is self-contained because _content_tokens already expands both sides symmetrically.

